/*! @brief XmlText object represents the text content of an element or attribute

XmlText nodes represent a series of plain text in XML documents. Because plain text appears in elements and attributes of XML, XmlText nodes usually appear as child nodes of XmlElement nodes and XmlAttr nodes.

XmlText nodes inherit the XmlCharacterData interface, and the text content of XmlText nodes can be accessed through the data property inherited from XmlCharacterData interface or the nadevalue property inherited from XmlNode interface.

XmlText nodes can be manipulated using methods inherited from XmlCharacterData or the splitText() method defined by the XmlText interface itself. Use XmlDocument's createTextNode to create a new XmlText node.

XmlText nodes have no child nodes.

For methods to delete empty XmlText nodes from the document's subtree and merge adjacent XmlText nodes, see the XmlNode.normalize method.
*/
interface XmlText : XmlCharacterData
{
    /*! @brief Split the text node into two nodes according to the specified offset

     This method will split the XmlText node into two nodes at the specified offset. The original XmlText node will be modified to contain the text content before the position specified by offset (but not including the text content). A new XmlText node will be created to hold all characters from the offset position (including the character at that position) to the end of the original characters. The new XmlText node is the return value of this method. In addition, if the original XmlText node has a parentNode, the new XmlText node will be inserted into this parent node, immediately after the original node.

     The XmlCDATASection interface inherits the XmlText interface, and XmlCDATASection nodes can also use this method, but the newly created node is an XmlCDATASection node, not an XmlText node.
     @param offset Specifies where to split the text node. The starting value starts at 0
     @return The Text node split from the current node
    */
    XmlText splitText(Integer offset);
};